==English==
{{wikipedia}}
[[File:Chopsueywithrice.jpg|thumb]]

===Etymology===
From {{bor|en|yue|-}} {{zh-l|雜碎|zaap<sup>6</sup> seoi<sup>3</sup>|mixed and broken}}.

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~|head=[[chop]] suey}}

# {{lb|en|US|Philippines}} A [[stir-fried]] vegetable dish, served with pieces of beef or pork in a semi-thick sauce, and often soy sauce.
# {{lb|en|Canada|and|British}} Steamed [[bean sprout]]s served in a semi-thick sauce, and mixed with a choice of meat and/or vegetables.
#* {{quote-book|en|author=w:Clarissa Dickson Wright|title=w:A History of English Food|location=London|publisher=[[w:Random House|Random House Books]]|year=2011|page=455|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/historyofenglish0000dick/page/455/mode/1up|isbn=9781905211852|passage=To someone who had spent part of their youth visiting a grandmother in Singapore, a lot of the food they served didn’t seem particularly Chinese to me: the flavour of '''chop suey''' with its chicken or pork or prawns, or chow mein, or the omeletty foo yung, never seemed quite right.}}

